# The live page is compared to this file byte for byte - preflight does it, and so does the itch-zip
# check - so the working copy has to stay LF whatever core.autocrlf says. It is true on this machine,
# and a plain `git checkout -- index.html` rewrote the page as CRLF once (65,971 bytes became 66,966)
# while git went on reporting the tree clean. Every byte-identity check then disagreed with a page that
# was perfectly fine. Pin the eol here so a checkout can never produce that again.
*.html text eol=lf

# manifest.json and favicon.svg are in the itch zip, and the zip check compares all eight files byte
# for byte - not just index.html, which is what the pin above was written for. So a checkout that
# rewrote either of those as CRLF would report "stale: manifest.json" about a file that is correct,
# the same false alarm, one line short of being fixed. The repack itself already forces LF
# (core.autocrlf=false in the archive command), so the zip side was never the half at risk.
*.json text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
# Not in the zip, but the same class: the suite reads index.html line by line, and nothing in this
# repo wants CRLF.
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
